1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:02,884
I don't read the code that my agent writes.

2
00:00:03,406 --> 00:00:05,060
Haven't, for months.

3
00:00:05,550 --> 00:00:08,932
Every feature on a platform that real people pay for.

4
00:00:09,646 --> 00:00:13,540
I describe it, the agent builds it, and I ship it.

5
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:16,452
And be honest, neither do you.

6
00:00:17,120 --> 00:00:19,588
Nobody can read AI code anymore.

7
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:22,916
Not because it's bad, but because there's too much of it.

8
00:00:23,246 --> 00:00:26,820
Written too fast by something that never gets tired.

9
00:00:27,342 --> 00:00:28,100
Watch this.

10
00:00:28,494 --> 00:00:31,812
One file and 11 other files depend on it.

11
00:00:32,462 --> 00:00:34,884
You would never guess that from a folder tree.

12
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:37,316
That's the actual problem.

13
00:00:37,998 --> 00:00:39,120
The code works.

14
00:00:39,440 --> 00:00:44,196
You just can't change anything without breaking something you didn't know existed.

15
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:47,120
And that's me on my own project.

16
00:00:47,406 --> 00:00:48,612
Now put a team on it.

17
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:58,244
Four people, all shipping with agents, nobody reading anyone's output, then someone new joins and inherits all of it from everyone.

18
00:00:58,734 --> 00:01:01,572
Ask the team what breaks if you change this file.

19
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:03,428
Nobody knows.

20
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:04,960
Not one person.

21
00:01:05,966 --> 00:01:08,420
And no, this isn't a vibe coding video.

22
00:01:08,720 --> 00:01:11,556
Not reading the code doesn't mean not checking it.

23
00:01:12,046 --> 00:01:13,440
So hold that thought.

24
00:01:13,614 --> 00:01:18,500
Hi, I'm Adrian, and today you're building the thing that fixes it.

25
00:01:18,766 --> 00:01:20,640
It's called Cartograph.

26
00:01:20,846 --> 00:01:27,524
Point it at any GitHub repo, and it turns the entire code base into a map that you can click through.

27
00:01:27,918 --> 00:01:31,428
Select any file and see exactly what breaks if you touch it.

28
00:01:31,694 --> 00:01:38,532
Get an explanation of any module written from the files it actually connects to with every file name clickable.

29
00:01:39,040 --> 00:01:43,108
Or just ask it a question, where does auth get enforced?

30
00:01:43,438 --> 00:01:50,372
And watch an agent walk the real graph step by step and come back with an answer you can actually verify.

31
00:01:50,640 --> 00:01:54,308
And there's just one rule that makes all of it trustworthy.

32
00:01:54,640 --> 00:01:58,160
Every line on that map comes from parsing real code.

33
00:01:58,414 --> 00:02:02,468
So it isn't guessed or summarized, it is parsed.

34
00:02:02,798 --> 00:02:07,396
The AI is only allowed to explain what the parser already proved.

35
00:02:07,694 --> 00:02:12,708
Because let's be honest, AI will happily invent connections that don't exist.

36
00:02:13,262 --> 00:02:17,860
And a map that's 90% right is worse than having no map at all.

37
00:02:18,126 --> 00:02:21,188
Because you can tell which 10% is lying.

38
00:02:21,520 --> 00:02:23,044
And this is not a toy.

39
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:28,356
Real sign-in, teams, a database that enforces who sees what.

40
00:02:28,846 --> 00:02:33,636
You map a code base once, and everyone who joins after you starts from the map.

41
00:02:33,966 --> 00:02:39,524
That's the difference between a script that you wrote for yourself and something you can actually charge for.

42
00:02:39,840 --> 00:02:42,820
And here's the part that I want you to stay for.

43
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:48,960
Somewhere in this build, the AI gives me an answer that sounds completely right.

44
00:02:49,614 --> 00:02:53,200
Confident, well written, and even specific.

45
00:02:53,600 --> 00:02:54,948
And it's wrong.

46
00:02:55,150 --> 00:02:56,996
I don't catch it by reading it.

47
00:02:57,582 --> 00:02:59,236
I catch it with a number.

48
00:02:59,630 --> 00:03:04,480
And by the end, you'll know how to put that number on anything you build with AI.

49
00:03:04,814 --> 00:03:06,084
Next.js in TypeScript.

50
00:03:06,766 --> 00:03:08,388
TSMorph parses the code.

51
00:03:09,006 --> 00:03:10,884
ReactFlow draws the map.

52
00:03:11,502 --> 00:03:13,188
Clerk runs the teams.

53
00:03:13,742 --> 00:03:15,460
Superbase holds the data.

54
00:03:16,110 --> 00:03:18,660
Langsmith proves the AI isn't lying.

55
00:03:19,086 --> 00:03:21,156
And CodeRabbit reviews the code.

56
00:03:21,742 --> 00:03:23,876
Let's go read the code that I've been ignoring.

57
00:03:34,094 --> 00:03:38,980
Before we write our first line of code, one word needs fixing.

58
00:03:39,344 --> 00:03:41,508
Because it's the thing you're gonna build.

59
00:03:42,094 --> 00:03:42,692
Agent.

60
00:03:42,958 --> 00:03:46,436
Here's what most of us have built and called AI.

61
00:03:47,214 --> 00:03:51,012
Get a key, send some text, and get some text back.

62
00:03:51,534 --> 00:03:52,900
15 lines of code.

63
00:03:53,326 --> 00:03:54,724
That's not an agent.

64
00:03:55,118 --> 00:03:56,144
That's a phone call.

65
00:03:56,304 --> 00:03:59,812
One question and one answer from whatever the model already knew.

66
00:04:00,366 --> 00:04:01,060
End of story.

67
00:04:01,486 --> 00:04:03,108
But now the real thing.

68
00:04:03,822 --> 00:04:11,268
When your coding agent reads six files before answering you, here's the part that I've seen some developers get wrong.

69
00:04:12,142 --> 00:04:13,476
It's not the model doing it.

70
00:04:14,158 --> 00:04:16,548
The model can't open a file.

71
00:04:16,974 --> 00:04:20,324
It can't run a command and can't search anything.

72
00:04:20,782 --> 00:04:22,500
It produces text.

73
00:04:22,830 --> 00:04:24,384
And that's the entire job.

74
00:04:24,654 --> 00:04:29,860
What it produces is a piece of text that says, I need to see this file.

75
00:04:30,606 --> 00:04:36,228
Something around it reads that, goes and gets that file, and feeds it back in.

76
00:04:36,846 --> 00:04:39,172
Then the model decides the next step.

77
00:04:39,758 --> 00:04:41,668
And again, and again.

78
00:04:42,894 --> 00:04:45,572
That loop is the agent.

79
00:04:46,094 --> 00:04:48,900
The model is just the part that decides.

80
00:04:49,934 --> 00:04:51,428
Nobody's scripted the order.

81
00:04:51,854 --> 00:05:02,020
It figures out what it needs one step at a time, which also means it's slower, it costs more, and it fails in ways that a single call can't.

82
00:05:02,318 --> 00:05:05,156
But there are some situations where you just need it.

83
00:05:05,678 --> 00:05:10,564
You reach for one when the question genuinely can't be answered in one shot.

84
00:05:11,086 --> 00:05:16,292
And what breaks if I change this file is exactly that kind of question.

85
00:05:16,464 --> 00:05:21,184
That's why this product, the one you'll build today, needs an agent.

86
00:05:21,614 --> 00:05:28,388
And a pretty cool part is that the agent that we'll build today won't run inside our app at all.

87
00:05:28,942 --> 00:05:34,308
It's gonna be deployed somewhere else, and it calls back into us for what it needs.

88
00:05:34,766 --> 00:05:41,284
Remember that, as it's the last thing we build, but also the best thing we build in this course.

89
00:05:41,584 --> 00:05:42,144
Right.

90
00:05:42,384 --> 00:05:45,604
But here's what building with agents actually looks like.

91
00:05:46,286 --> 00:05:50,084
You write a prompt, you run it, and it looks good.

92
00:05:50,574 --> 00:05:56,228
Maybe you tweak it a bit, add an additional instruction or two, or maybe cut one.

93
00:05:56,846 --> 00:05:57,796
Is it better now?

94
00:05:58,144 --> 00:05:59,524
You don't know.

95
00:05:59,984 --> 00:06:06,864
You ran it twice, it looks fine both times, you switch to a cheaper model to save money, still good.

96
00:06:07,024 --> 00:06:10,064
Well, no idea, as it reads fine.

97
00:06:10,304 --> 00:06:13,732
And that's the actual problem with AI features.

98
00:06:14,254 --> 00:06:15,492
Not that they break.

99
00:06:16,064 --> 00:06:18,500
Regular code breaks loudly.

100
00:06:18,944 --> 00:06:22,148
Stack trace, red line, you go ahead and fix it.

101
00:06:23,086 --> 00:06:25,024
This never breaks.

102
00:06:25,264 --> 00:06:28,356
It just quietly gets a bit worse.

103
00:06:28,864 --> 00:06:33,028
Or it stays mediocre for three months and nothing tells you.

104
00:06:33,518 --> 00:06:36,132
Because the output always looks fine.

105
00:06:36,590 --> 00:06:38,948
It's well written, whether it's right

106
00:06:39,664 --> 00:06:40,304
or not.

107
00:06:40,654 --> 00:06:42,916
So fine, you'll read it yourself.

108
00:06:43,502 --> 00:06:45,348
On one file, sure.

109
00:06:46,030 --> 00:06:46,704
On a hundred?

110
00:06:46,958 --> 00:06:49,604
And then will you do it again when you change the prompt?

111
00:06:49,998 --> 00:06:52,304
Or once again when you swap the model?

112
00:06:52,494 --> 00:06:54,916
That's where reading stops working.

113
00:06:55,278 --> 00:07:00,292
So instead, there's three things, and we'll build all three today.

114
00:07:00,910 --> 00:07:07,108
First, you record every call what went in, what came out, and what it cost.

115
00:07:08,014 --> 00:07:14,340
Second, you build a set of questions where you already know the right answer.

116
00:07:15,022 --> 00:07:18,400
And third, you score the AI against them.

117
00:07:18,670 --> 00:07:22,404
So changing a prompt gives you a number instead of a feeling.

118
00:07:22,894 --> 00:07:24,452
That's Langsmith.

119
00:07:24,814 --> 00:07:27,204
And it isn't this lang chain thing.

120
00:07:27,598 --> 00:07:29,828
There's no lang chain in this project.

121
00:07:30,126 --> 00:07:34,560
We call the SDK directly, and Langsmith just watches.

122
00:07:35,054 --> 00:07:37,444
Actually, do yourself a favor right now.

123
00:07:37,966 --> 00:07:40,164
There's a Langsmith link in the description.

124
00:07:40,686 --> 00:07:42,468
Open it, create an account.

125
00:07:42,798 --> 00:07:46,148
It takes about a minute, and the tier we're using today is free.

126
00:07:46,702 --> 00:07:55,652
Because when the first model call lands later on in the video, every trace, every database, and every score you just saw runs through it.

127
00:07:56,142 --> 00:08:00,260
And I'd rather you set it up now than pause mid-build to make an account.

128
00:08:00,720 --> 00:08:02,244
But here's a hard one.

129
00:08:02,894 --> 00:08:05,764
Where do the right answers come from?

130
00:08:06,160 --> 00:08:09,316
For most AI products, there aren't any.

131
00:08:10,030 --> 00:08:12,516
A chatbot has no correct reply.

132
00:08:12,910 --> 00:08:17,092
So people end up using another AI to grade the first AI.

133
00:08:17,550 --> 00:08:21,920
Which works, sort of, but you're still trusting a model.

134
00:08:22,240 --> 00:08:24,132
This project is different.

135
00:08:24,558 --> 00:08:26,628
And it's half the reason I picked it.

136
00:08:26,960 --> 00:08:32,560
We're parsing real code, which means the app is already holding the ground truth.

137
00:08:32,782 --> 00:08:38,404
Every file that exists, every connection that's actually out there, nobody decided any of it.

138
00:08:38,862 --> 00:08:40,388
It came out of the parser.

139
00:08:40,910 --> 00:08:45,760
So when the AI mentions a file, I can check it against the real list.

140
00:08:46,510 --> 00:08:48,612
Instantly, with no judgment call.

141
00:08:48,974 --> 00:08:52,720
The right answers come free from the code itself.

142
00:08:52,974 --> 00:08:54,244
Most AI features,

143
00:08:55,054 --> 00:08:56,292
you just have to trust.

144
00:08:57,198 --> 00:08:59,268
This one, you can check.

145
00:08:59,470 --> 00:09:01,412
So that's what we're building today.

146
00:09:01,680 --> 00:09:03,204
But there's one more thing.

147
00:09:03,760 --> 00:09:05,828
And it's how we build it.

148
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:09,188
In 2012, you get paid to write code.

149
00:09:09,838 --> 00:09:10,692
That was the job.

150
00:09:11,214 --> 00:09:14,372
Know the syntax, write it faster, ship more of it.

151
00:09:15,182 --> 00:09:17,680
But writing the code is the cheap part now.

152
00:09:18,030 --> 00:09:22,980
What you get paid for now is being the one who decides what to build.

153
00:09:23,406 --> 00:09:27,140
Who spots the moment the thing in front of you is subtly wrong.

154
00:09:27,566 --> 00:09:34,084
Who knows enough about databases and security and architecture to catch it before it ships.

155
00:09:35,310 --> 00:09:38,244
You don't have to be the expert in all of it.

156
00:09:38,734 --> 00:09:44,420
You just have to know enough to drive it and enough to tell it when it's confidently wrong.

157
00:09:44,720 --> 00:09:48,932
So every feature here starts as a short file in plain English.

158
00:09:49,518 --> 00:09:54,308
What it does, what it's not allowed to do, and how I'll know it worked.

159
00:09:55,150 --> 00:09:59,364
Then an agent turns it into working code and I check it.

160
00:09:59,854 --> 00:10:05,680
No pseudocode or file names, just a clear description of the thing that I want.

161
00:10:06,574 --> 00:10:08,900
And one honest thing before we start.

162
00:10:09,486 --> 00:10:13,860
This loop I just described, spec build, check.

163
00:10:14,350 --> 00:10:17,220
Well, you're going to watch me run it manually today.

164
00:10:17,840 --> 00:10:21,380
But that same loop exists as a part of a system.

165
00:10:21,966 --> 00:10:30,308
Nine skills that you install into any project, scope that challenges the idea before any code, develop that builds it,

166
00:10:30,894 --> 00:10:34,468
and check turns on a different model to review what was built.

167
00:10:34,640 --> 00:10:36,612
They're open source, free.

168
00:10:37,038 --> 00:10:39,332
I'll leave the link below so you can install them today.

169
00:10:39,680 --> 00:10:42,480
And there's also the agenc engineering course.

170
00:10:42,640 --> 00:10:45,668
So what's the agenc engineering course about?

171
00:10:46,542 --> 00:10:49,668
Well, the course is the part you can't install.

172
00:10:50,606 --> 00:10:51,460
The judgment.

173
00:10:52,206 --> 00:10:59,588
Which skill to reach for, what to answer when it stops and asks, and how to catch the AI drifting at loop 3.

174
00:10:59,790 --> 00:11:01,924
And the scenarios that nobody teaches.

175
00:11:02,446 --> 00:11:07,492
Adding features to a product that's already live, and the one this whole video exists for.

176
00:11:07,982 --> 00:11:10,788
80,000 lines of code that you didn't write.

177
00:11:11,086 --> 00:11:13,156
That's the agentic engineering course.

178
00:11:13,390 --> 00:11:18,560
It's live now with a special discount if you come from this video, and the link is in the description.

179
00:11:18,798 --> 00:11:20,644
And that's all I'll say about it for now.

180
00:11:21,454 --> 00:11:23,588
All right, let's set up the project.

181
00:11:25,710 --> 00:11:29,668
Let's dive right in and set up a fresh Next.js project.

182
00:11:30,318 --> 00:11:33,956
We could get AI to do it, but it's just one simple command.

183
00:11:34,158 --> 00:11:42,144
So type pmpm create next app at latest, give it a name, and accept all the default options

184
00:11:42,464 --> 00:11:43,300
and run it.

185
00:11:43,854 --> 00:11:45,348
This will create a new folder,

186
00:11:46,478 --> 00:11:50,784
download Next.js and all the necessary packages, and prepare it for you to run it.

187
00:11:50,958 --> 00:11:58,212
As soon as it's done, simply drag and drop that folder into your code editor, whether that's VS Code, cursor, or anything else.

188
00:11:58,638 --> 00:12:03,556
When you open it up, it'll look something like this: a basic Next.js application.

189
00:12:04,494 --> 00:12:10,532
But before anything gets built, the agent needs to know what it's building and why.

190
00:12:11,086 --> 00:12:14,148
Because if you don't tell it, it decides for itself.

191
00:12:14,638 --> 00:12:16,420
And it decides reasonably.

192
00:12:16,864 --> 00:12:22,144
It just decides something you didn't ask for, and you only find out three prompts later.

193
00:12:22,446 --> 00:12:26,276
So go ahead and delete this current ClaudeMD, which is more or less empty.

194
00:12:26,830 --> 00:12:35,184
And in the video kit link down in the description, I'll provide you with two new files, the updated ClaudeMD as well as the Project Doc

195
00:12:35,584 --> 00:12:36,004
MD.

196
00:12:36,302 --> 00:12:39,264
So get them and let's open them side by side.

197
00:12:39,504 --> 00:12:42,944
These two are pre-spec files about the project.

198
00:12:43,278 --> 00:12:49,668
Project Docs is the idea that you wrote for the AI context, and ClaudeMD are the rules for the agent.

199
00:12:50,158 --> 00:12:55,104
Both of these files are the agent context and the rules of the application and writing code.

200
00:12:55,344 --> 00:13:03,396
One describes the app, that's the project doc, what it is, who it's for, and every decision I've made with the reasons attached.

201
00:13:03,790 --> 00:13:05,380
The other is the rules.

