00:13:22,542 --> 00:13:27,108
The project doc can go into a special docs folder, just so we keep everything tidy.

206
00:13:27,406 --> 00:13:29,664
Okay, so let's go through it in a bit more detail.

207
00:13:29,838 --> 00:13:32,624
Maybe we can even open it in preview so it's a bit easier to read.

208
00:13:32,814 --> 00:13:35,744
We start a bit with the idea of the project.

209
00:13:36,142 --> 00:13:36,864
Cartograph.

210
00:13:37,024 --> 00:13:41,348
The product decisions behind this build, written before it starts, with the reasoning attached.

211
00:13:42,062 --> 00:13:42,704
What is it?

212
00:13:42,958 --> 00:13:48,772
Sign in with GitHub, paste a public TypeScript or JavaScript repository URL, and get a map of it.

213
00:13:49,070 --> 00:13:54,304
Folders are boxes, imports as lines between them, and open up a folder into its files.

214
00:13:54,574 --> 00:13:59,744
Select the file and see what imports it and what breaks two levels out if it changes.

215
00:13:59,984 --> 00:14:05,412
Ask for an explanation and get a paragraph written from that file and its real neighbors.

216
00:14:06,030 --> 00:14:10,864
Then a panel where you can ask the repository a question and watch it look the answer up.

217
00:14:11,024 --> 00:14:14,756
We further go ahead and explain the problem that I already told you about in the intro.

218
00:14:15,118 --> 00:14:16,836
And then this is an interesting part.

219
00:14:17,344 --> 00:14:18,624
Who is it for?

220
00:14:18,894 --> 00:14:20,548
It starts with one paragraph.

221
00:14:20,878 --> 00:14:24,164
Someone opening a code base they didn't write.

222
00:14:24,558 --> 00:14:28,864
Usually one they built with an agent and can't hold in their head anymore.

223
00:14:29,024 --> 00:14:31,396
That's the line that kills features later.

224
00:14:31,744 --> 00:14:40,132
Because every time an agent wants to add a score, a rating or a review panel, it asks, does the person I described want that?

225
00:14:40,462 --> 00:14:42,212
Most likely the answer is no.

226
00:14:42,446 --> 00:14:45,060
They simply want to know what depends on this file.

227
00:14:45,550 --> 00:14:48,548
And then there's the part that most developers skip.

228
00:14:49,294 --> 00:14:51,424
Why this hasn't stuck before?

229
00:14:51,822 --> 00:14:54,052
What happened to everyone who tried this already?

230
00:14:54,766 --> 00:14:58,916
Code C built almost exactly this with funding, but it's gone.

231
00:14:59,758 --> 00:15:04,064
Same thing happened with source trail, ran for years and got archived.

232
00:15:04,144 --> 00:15:05,636
And then there's this line.

233
00:15:05,902 --> 00:15:08,196
It is a bet and it should be stated as one.

234
00:15:08,526 --> 00:15:14,304
So when something goes sideways later, I can tell a bad implementation from a bad assumption.

235
00:15:14,544 --> 00:15:22,980
Then we have a part of what is in and what is not in, which you can read as scope and out of scope and why.

236
00:15:23,184 --> 00:15:25,636
The in list is barely more than bullets.

237
00:15:26,062 --> 00:15:29,252
The not list has a reason attached to every item.

