1107
01:25:48,526 --> 01:25:51,780
Phase 6 committed, pushed, and the PR opened.

1108
01:25:53,646 --> 01:25:59,652
Everything we've built so far has been working against repository data that we put into the project ourselves.

1109
01:26:00,270 --> 01:26:02,948
Now we're replacing that with a real flow.

1110
01:26:03,534 --> 01:26:11,392
We want to be able to paste a GitHub URL, fetch the repo, parse it, store the result, and take us straight to the map.

1111
01:26:11,566 --> 01:26:16,512
Once that works, the temporary parser output that we've been using can finally go away.

1112
01:26:16,672 --> 01:26:19,472
For that, we'll use the phase 07

1113
01:26:20,750 --> 01:26:25,380
with the goal of pasting a URL and watching your own repository become a map.

1114
01:26:25,632 --> 01:26:32,192
The pipeline here has four main stages fetch, select, parse, and store.

1115
01:26:32,494 --> 01:26:38,948
We fetch public repositories as archives, so we don't need repository permissions or need to store a GitHub token.

1116
01:26:39,072 --> 01:26:44,996
We also keep the commit the analysis came from, because later we'll need to know if the repository has changed.

1117
01:26:45,472 --> 01:26:49,392
And we only want to have one analysis per repository.

1118
01:26:49,678 --> 01:26:57,444
If you paste the repo you've already analyzed, or that somebody from your organization has already analyzed, we don't need to create another copy.

1119
01:26:57,712 --> 01:26:59,908
We immediately take you to the existing one.

1120
01:27:00,590 --> 01:27:05,872
And if you want to actually rerun it, well, that's a separate action from inside the analysis.

1121
01:27:06,094 --> 01:27:08,708
As usual, let's also explore the constraints.

1122
01:27:09,774 --> 01:27:14,352
Live progress is driven from the database, not from browser polling.

1123
01:27:14,734 --> 01:27:20,836
As the pipeline moves from one stage to the next, the database publishes that change and the page listens for it.

1124
01:27:21,422 --> 01:27:24,580
So the browser isn't constantly asking, are we done yet?

1125
01:27:24,752 --> 01:27:28,752
Also, the channel has to be declared before anything can publish to it.

1126
01:27:28,942 --> 01:27:30,788
And this is an easy one to miss.

1127
01:27:31,374 --> 01:27:38,148
If the real-time channel isn't registered properly, the pipeline can keep running while the progress page never hears about it.

1128
01:27:38,414 --> 01:27:44,352
So we need to define that channel as part of the migration instead of relying on it to magically exist.

1129
01:27:44,654 --> 01:27:47,332
And who may subscribe is also a policy.

1130
01:27:48,014 --> 01:27:54,212
Another organization's analysis channel is not subscribable for the same reason its rows aren't selectable.

1131
01:27:54,638 --> 01:27:58,192
The progress stream is protected the same way that the data is.

1132
01:27:58,446 --> 01:28:02,884
One team shouldn't be able to subscribe to another team's analysis progress.

1133
01:28:03,072 --> 01:28:07,204
Also, a run that dies without failing has to be visible too.

1134
01:28:07,726 --> 01:28:10,372
We need to handle two different failure cases.

1135
01:28:10,830 --> 01:28:14,148
If the pipeline throws an error, we need to record what happens.

1136
01:28:14,574 --> 01:28:19,072
But if the process disappears halfway through, there may be no error to catch at all.

1137
01:28:19,342 --> 01:28:26,180
So if a run stays unfinished for too long, the dashboard marks it as stale instead of pretending that it's still working.

1138
01:28:26,894 --> 01:28:30,352
This is a pretty big phase, so I'm going to build it in two passes.

1139
01:28:30,638 --> 01:28:36,592
Let's open up the agent and tell it to read Clotmd and docs specs phase 07.

1140
01:28:37,582 --> 01:28:44,644
And we want to start with fetching a repo and running it through the parser into storage with no UI yet.

1141
01:28:45,072 --> 01:28:46,788
That we're going to do later on.

1142
01:28:47,918 --> 01:28:51,748
So let's run it and let's see if it has any questions for us before it builds it.

1143
01:28:51,920 --> 01:28:54,308
Before building, three things need our decision.

1144
01:28:54,894 --> 01:28:57,124
Writer, package, and see the rows.

1145
01:28:57,520 --> 01:29:00,836
First, how should the run write rows?

1146
01:29:01,200 --> 01:29:07,300
A clerk session token lasts 60 seconds, and the request is over before a real repo finished parsing.

1147
01:29:07,520 --> 01:29:10,400
So writing with a user's token won't last the whole run.

1148
01:29:10,766 --> 01:29:14,788
I'd use the Superbase Secret key kept to the server-only writer module.

1149
01:29:14,990 --> 01:29:21,188
The org ID still comes from the session token when you submit, and every read stays under the existing policies.

1150
01:29:21,520 --> 01:29:32,560
This is important because there's a difference from the user-facing database requests that we've built so far and this one, which needs to write a lot of analysis data in the background to the server.

1151
01:29:32,814 --> 01:29:37,764
So it uses a server-side secret credential rather than the signed-in user's browser session.

1152
01:29:38,254 --> 01:29:44,640
That means that the pipeline itself has to be very careful to attach the correct organization to everything it writes.

1153
01:29:44,942 --> 01:29:49,360
So for this to work, we have to add the Superbase Secret Key to ENVLocal.

1154
01:29:49,550 --> 01:29:52,804
So head over to Superbase, under your project,

1155
01:29:53,520 --> 01:29:55,076
go under settings,

1156
01:29:55,886 --> 01:29:56,996
API, keys,

1157
01:29:57,680 --> 01:29:59,076
scroll to the bottom.

1158
01:29:59,502 --> 01:30:02,628
and under secret keys, you can copy this API key

1159
01:30:03,600 --> 01:30:09,920
and head over into.env.local and paste it as it says right. here.

1160
01:30:10,222 --> 01:30:12,196
That's going to be under Superbase.

1161
01:30:12,400 --> 01:30:15,440
And it told us to call it Superbase Secret Key.

1162
01:30:15,520 --> 01:30:16,900
So that's exactly what I'll do.

1163
01:30:17,422 --> 01:30:20,640
There we go, and it's right here at the top beside Superbase.

1164
01:30:20,846 --> 01:30:23,396
So we can say this is something that we have already added.

1165
01:30:24,238 --> 01:30:25,040
Now package.

1166
01:30:25,294 --> 01:30:30,340
GitHub archives are tar.gzip and node can't unpack tar on its own.

1167
01:30:30,670 --> 01:30:32,240
Can I install the tar package?

1168
01:30:32,400 --> 01:30:33,444
Yep, definitely.

1169
01:30:33,934 --> 01:30:43,940
And about seeding the rows, the seed has two analyses for Next.js and two for UI that breaks one analysis per repository, and none of the seed rows have files behind them.

1170
01:30:44,334 --> 01:30:48,560
Can I delete the seed rows and add a unique constraint on analysis per project?

1171
01:30:48,750 --> 01:30:50,640
Yep, that's exactly what we want to do.

1172
01:30:50,830 --> 01:30:57,636
From this point onward, the dashboard only shows real analyses, and the database enforces one per repo.

1173
01:30:58,414 --> 01:31:05,188
Wonderful. I appreciate these types of questions because they push the project in the right direction instead of derailing it further.

1174
01:31:05,360 --> 01:31:09,316
And after about a couple of minutes, the pipeline works from the terminal.

1175
01:31:09,870 --> 01:31:12,036
It tested everything so we didn't have to.

1176
01:31:12,430 --> 01:31:16,196
Fetch, select, parse, and store, writing real rows.

1177
01:31:16,526 --> 01:31:19,236
No UI is wired to it yet, so nothing is committed.

1178
01:31:19,758 --> 01:31:20,640
It runs end-to-end.

1179
01:31:20,960 --> 01:31:25,828
You can run PMPM analyze and paste the public repository URL from GitHub.

1180
01:31:25,920 --> 01:31:32,240
