02:32:13,696 --> 02:32:18,468
The entire point of the app is catching AI hallucinated file paths.

1897
02:32:18,798 --> 02:32:22,148
And CodeRabbit found two holes in the catcher itself.

1898
02:32:22,766 --> 02:32:25,668
A relative path like a client TS

1899
02:32:26,574 --> 02:32:30,496
could match the tail of a completely different file and pass as real.

1900
02:32:31,150 --> 02:32:36,548
And any fake path hidden behind a backtick span with spaces just got skipped entirely.

1901
02:32:37,102 --> 02:32:41,616
So my hallucination detector had two ways to hallucinate a clean result.

1902
02:32:42,030 --> 02:32:46,084
And that's exactly the kind of bug you never find by eyeballing a diff.

1903
02:32:46,576 --> 02:32:48,976
Because it isn't really a bug.

1904
02:32:49,230 --> 02:32:52,496
For example, this self-importing roles.ts file.

1905
02:32:52,750 --> 02:32:53,988
This one's sneakier.

1906
02:32:54,446 --> 02:33:04,548
A file that imports itself showed up in its own neighbor list with its role attached, which means that my eval was asking the model a question that contained the answer.

1907
02:33:04,910 --> 02:33:07,936
So the numbers were lying to me and I had no idea.

1908
02:33:08,238 --> 02:33:11,876
Similar thing happens with the shared TS and Pat's TS right here.

1909
02:33:12,256 --> 02:33:13,988
But here's my favorite part.

1910
02:33:14,576 --> 02:33:18,416
The sixth comment said to switch over to next ENV.

1911
02:33:18,670 --> 02:33:20,548
Claude code didn't just apply it.

1912
02:33:20,942 --> 02:33:24,772
It checked whether my ENV actually uses variable expansion.

1913
02:33:25,646 --> 02:33:28,772
Saw that it doesn't, and skipped the fix with a reason.

1914
02:33:28,976 --> 02:33:30,244
And that's the whole game.

1915
02:33:30,670 --> 02:33:33,476
Review comments are inputs and not orders.

1916
02:33:33,776 --> 02:33:38,436
The agent verified a finding against the repo and rejected it.

1917
02:33:38,736 --> 02:33:41,028
Because it wasn't the right fix for this project.

1918
02:33:41,230 --> 02:33:45,536
And we're now close to the end of building this amazing project.

1919
02:33:45,776 --> 02:33:48,676
Something that before would take months to build.

1920
02:33:48,976 --> 02:33:50,884
We just built it in the afternoon.

1921
02:33:51,536 --> 02:33:59,296
But this part, the review, the verification, deciding what's actually worth fixing, that's the real job now.

1922
02:33:59,598 --> 02:34:03,536
In other words, the bottleneck moved from writing to validating.

1923
02:34:03,694 --> 02:34:05,536
